I don't fully follow what the "although" close is modifying--the only
dwarven armor in the game _is_ mithril, and mithril isn't good for
casters (except in the sense of "it protects them while wearing it,
and they can take it off if they need to cast").

I think you're trying to say "Until you can find dragon scale mail,
for casters, studded leather is best.  For others, dwarven mithril is
best but elvish mithril is pretty close".  If so, that's reasonable
(although it's sometimes worth considering wearing heavy armor as a
low-level caster and throwing daggers/darts/etc until you find other
ways to get decent AC).

In particular, with a +5 COMR that's AC 4 right there.  Throw in, say,
iron shoes, a dwarvish shield, dwarvish mithril coat, a dwarvish helm,
and leather gloves and you're looking at AC -9 right there without
much luck--and normally by the time you reach the quest you'll have
managed at least a couple protection buys even if you're not a
racketeer.  Say -12 or so for an average game if you've got that COMR,
slightly worse if you've got some good value (speed boots, helm of
telepathy, etc) that's not quite as protective, or if you've gotten
your luck up enough to start #twoweapon.  Maybe you get unlucky with a
few finds but -4 is really poor.

So yeah, you should work on getting more free AC early on.  It's
normal for me to come back from Minetown at around -4 (I do Minetown
before Sokoban, though I may do a couple main-dungeon levels before
entering the mines if I'm not at high enough HP or low enough AC), and
I don't do the protection racket or anything else unusual--and that's
without finding anything like a +5 cloak.

